lane: adaptive-parts
why: today's tables say the right buffer depends on the producer
  count -- one ring wins at one producer, a relaxed one wins by 19.6x
  at sixteen -- and the caller usually does not know that number when
  the channel is made. Contract and boundedness stay the caller's
  (they are semantics); the PART COUNT is performance and, crucially,
  invisible through the contract: the laws promise per-producer order,
  no loss, no duplication, and say NOTHING about order between
  producers.
how: AdaptiveFifo -- parts created on demand, one per producer thread,
  up to a cap. No element ever migrates, which is what keeps the
  lock-free structure out of the stop-the-world problem the obvious
  "detect contention and move" design would have. One producer sees
  one part and pays a one-part scan.
  New constructor only: Queues.strong[A].adaptive(maxParts). Nothing
  existing changes.
the window to close, named up front: `seal` must reach every part, and
  a NEW part can appear after close begins. Parts must stop being
  created once `closing`, and that must be a law rather than an
  argument -- it is the same shape as the five defects this design
  already produced.
land only if it is no worse than one ring at one producer AND close to
  the relaxed lane at sixteen; otherwise withdraw and record why.
